{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1794914541721,"sessionID":"ses_e16c29ae3ffeLmKp3aWafWpYBG","part":{"type":"tool","tool":"read","callID":"call_ec5b47cae3434e4fa00fdc47","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"filePath":"/private/tmp/claude-502/-Users-alexander--claude-spares-spare-IwZtqC/50e3bfb1-34ed-4585-ad26-c678e31a5e93/scratchpad/agents-out2/amend-opencode/answer-questions/opencode-1/repo/ANSWERS.md","offset":1,"limit":2000},"output":"<path>/private/tmp/claude-502/-Users-alexander--claude-spares-spare-IwZtqC/50e3bfb1-34ed-4585-ad26-c678e31a5e93/scratchpad/agents-out2/amend-opencode/answer-questions/opencode-1/repo/ANSWERS.md</path>\n<type>file</type>\n<content>\n1: # Answers about the parcels code\n2: \n3: ## Q1\n4: \n5: Shipping is free for EU orders whose value, after discounts, is **€60.00 or more**. The default threshold is `FREE_SHIPPING_FROM = Decimal(\"60.00\")`, and the comparison is inclusive (`order_value >= FREE_SHIPPING_FROM`). Non-EU destinations are rejected before the free-shipping check. (`parcels/settings.py:14-15`, `parcels/quote.py:25-32`)\n6: \n7: The threshold can be changed without editing code by setting the `PARCELS_FREE_FROM` environment variable before starting the process, for example `PARCELS_FREE_FROM=75.00`. Settings are read from the environment once at import, so the application must be restarted (or the module reloaded) for the new value to take effect. (`parcels/settings.py:1-15`)\n8: \n9: ## Q2\n10: \n11: An order heavier than **31.5 kg** is not rejected: it is divided into the smallest whole number of parcels needed to keep each parcel within that limit. The parcels have equal weights, rounded up to the next gram; for example, 70 kg becomes three parcels of 23.334 kg. There is no maximum total order weight in this implementation, though a non-positive weight is invalid. (`parcels/settings.py:17`, `parcels/packing.py:11-22`)\n12: \n13: When the order is not eligible for free shipping, shipping is calculated separately for every resulting parcel and the parcel prices are added together. (`parcels/quote.py:17-22`, `parcels/quote.py:33`)\n14: \n15: ## Q3\n16: \n17: The default carrier is **PostNL**. Another carrier is selected without editing code by setting `PARCELS_CARRIER` to `dhl`, `ups`, or `postnl` before starting the process, for example `PARCELS_CARRIER=dhl`. The value is lower-cased; an unsupported value raises a `RuntimeError`. As with the other settings, the environment is read at import time, so a running process must be restarted to use a change. (`parcels/settings.py:1-12`)\n18: \n19: The configured carrier is used to select its tariff when calculating the price. (`parcels/quote.py:17-19`, `parcels/carriers.py:16-20`)\n20: \n21: ## Q4\n22: \n23: The result depends on the destination country. An order placed at 17:00 is after the 15:00 cutoff, so processing starts on the next calendar day—Saturday for a Friday order. The calculator then adds one handling day plus the destination's transit days, counting only Monday through Friday as working days. (`parcels/delivery.py:9-11`, `parcels/delivery.py:25-30`, `parcels/settings.py:18`)\n24: \n25: For a Friday order, the resulting delivery dates are:\n26: \n27: - **NL:** Tuesday of the following week (one transit day).\n28: - **BE, DE, or LU:** Wednesday of the following week (two transit days).\n29: - **Any other country:** Friday of the following week (the default four transit days). (`parcels/delivery.py:9-10`, `parcels/delivery.py:29-30`)\n30: \n31: **Weekends are accounted for:** the date calculation skips Saturday and Sunday. **Public holidays are not accounted for:** there is no holiday calendar, and a public holiday on a weekday is treated as a normal working day. (`parcels/delivery.py:14-22`)\n\n(End of file - total 31 lines)\n</content>","metadata":{"preview":"# Answers about the parcels code\n\n## Q1\n\nShipping is free for EU orders whose value, after discounts, is **€60.00 or more**. The default threshold is `FREE_SHIPPING_FROM = Decimal(\"60.00\")`, and the comparison is inclusive (`order_value >= FREE_SHIPPING_FROM`). Non-EU destinations are rejected before the free-shipping check. (`parcels/settings.py:14-15`, `parcels/quote.py:25-32`)\n\nThe threshold can be changed without editing code by setting the `PARCELS_FREE_FROM` environment variable before starting the process, for example `PARCELS_FREE_FROM=75.00`. Settings are read from the environment once at import, so the application must be restarted (or the module reloaded) for the new value to take effect. (`parcels/settings.py:1-15`)\n\n## Q2\n\nAn order heavier than **31.5 kg** is not rejected: it is divided into the smallest whole number of parcels needed to keep each parcel within that limit. The parcels have equal weights, rounded up to the next gram; for example, 70 kg becomes three parcels of 23.334 kg. There is no maximum total order weight in this implementation, though a non-positive weight is invalid. (`parcels/settings.py:17`, `parcels/packing.py:11-22`)\n\nWhen the order is not eligible for free shipping, shipping is calculated separately for every resulting parcel and the parcel prices are added together. (`parcels/quote.py:17-22`, `parcels/quote.py:33`)\n\n## Q3\n\nThe default carrier is **PostNL**. Another carrier is selected without editing code by setting `PARCELS_CARRIER` to `dhl`, `ups`, or `postnl` before starting the process, for example `PARCELS_CARRIER=dhl`. The value is lower-cased; an unsupported value raises a `RuntimeError`. As with the other settings, the environment is read at import time, so a running process must be restarted to use a change. (`parcels/settings.py:1-12`)\n\nThe configured carrier is used to select its tariff when calculating the price. (`parcels/quote.py:17-19`, `parcels/carriers.py:16-20`)\n","truncated":false,"loaded":[],"display":{"type":"file","path":"/private/tmp/claude-502/-Users-alexander--claude-spares-spare-IwZtqC/50e3bfb1-34ed-4585-ad26-c678e31a5e93/scratchpad/agents-out2/amend-opencode/answer-questions/opencode-1/repo/ANSWERS.md","text":"# Answers about the parcels code\n\n## Q1\n\nShipping is free for EU orders whose value, after discounts, is **€60.00 or more**. The default threshold is `FREE_SHIPPING_FROM = Decimal(\"60.00\")`, and the comparison is inclusive (`order_value >= FREE_SHIPPING_FROM`). Non-EU destinations are rejected before the free-shipping check. (`parcels/settings.py:14-15`, `parcels/quote.py:25-32`)\n\nThe threshold can be changed without editing code by setting the `PARCELS_FREE_FROM` environment variable before starting the process, for example `PARCELS_FREE_FROM=75.00`. Settings are read from the environment once at import, so the application must be restarted (or the module reloaded) for the new value to take effect. (`parcels/settings.py:1-15`)\n\n## Q2\n\nAn order heavier than **31.5 kg** is not rejected: it is divided into the smallest whole number of parcels needed to keep each parcel within that limit. The parcels have equal weights, rounded up to the next gram; for example, 70 kg becomes three parcels of 23.334 kg. There is no maximum total order weight in this implementation, though a non-positive weight is invalid. (`parcels/settings.py:17`, `parcels/packing.py:11-22`)\n\nWhen the order is not eligible for free shipping, shipping is calculated separately for every resulting parcel and the parcel prices are added together. (`parcels/quote.py:17-22`, `parcels/quote.py:33`)\n\n## Q3\n\nThe default carrier is **PostNL**. Another carrier is selected without editing code by setting `PARCELS_CARRIER` to `dhl`, `ups`, or `postnl` before starting the process, for example `PARCELS_CARRIER=dhl`. The value is lower-cased; an unsupported value raises a `RuntimeError`. As with the other settings, the environment is read at import time, so a running process must be restarted to use a change. (`parcels/settings.py:1-12`)\n\nThe configured carrier is used to select its tariff when calculating the price. (`parcels/quote.py:17-19`, `parcels/carriers.py:16-20`)\n\n## Q4\n\nThe result depends on the destination country. An order placed at 17:00 is after the 15:00 cutoff, so processing starts on the next calendar day—Saturday for a Friday order. The calculator then adds one handling day plus the destination's transit days, counting only Monday through Friday as working days. (`parcels/delivery.py:9-11`, `parcels/delivery.py:25-30`, `parcels/settings.py:18`)\n\nFor a Friday order, the resulting delivery dates are:\n\n- **NL:** Tuesday of the following week (one transit day).\n- **BE, DE, or LU:** Wednesday of the following week (two transit days).\n- **Any other country:** Friday of the following week (the default four transit days). (`parcels/delivery.py:9-10`, `parcels/delivery.py:29-30`)\n\n**Weekends are accounted for:** the date calculation skips Saturday and Sunday. **Public holidays are not accounted for:** there is no holiday calendar, and a public holiday on a weekday is treated as a normal working day. (`parcels/delivery.py:14-22`)","lineStart":1,"lineEnd":31,"totalLines":31,"truncated":false}},"title":"ANSWERS.md","time":{"start":1794914541715,"end":1794914541719}},"metadata":{"openrouter":{"reasoning_details":[{"type":"reasoning.text","text":"Need verify contents, citations validity. Read file. Maybe run test and git diff/status. Must explain nontrivial bash command before command. Test simple maybe text. \"I’ll verify...\" But tool commentary output visible; concise. We can say \"I’m running the existing unit tests and checking that only `ANSWERS.md` changed.\" Then parallel bash? Developer says when multiple bash calls independent single message multiple tools. Could call one bash chained? `python -m unittest discover -s tests && git status --short && git diff --check && git diff -- ANSWERS.md` but git diff won't show untracked file! `git status`. Read separately. We can run tests and git status in parallel separate tool calls in same message using multi_tool_use. Bash calls non-trivial? Explain. Also need read file in parallel perhaps. Use read and two bash. But functions.bash working directory. Test no changes. git diff --check won't inspect untracked. We can run unittest, git status --short. Also maybe use Python script to verify cited line paths? Not needed. Need ensure no code changed. Git status. Let's do read + tests/status parallel.","format":"unknown","index":0}]}},"id":"prt_1e93e600f001s7zamqgqehcjw7","sessionID":"ses_e16c29ae3ffeLmKp3aWafWpYBG","messageID":"msg_1e93e58a9001Vr0MJ8sOXBRnFr"}}
